# EXPECT: assigns enclosing name
#
# A `local` declaration that comes AFTER a READ of the same name.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is that `own` is shared with the CAPTURE refusal, so
# widening it to fix a false outward-mutation refusal silently suppressed a
# capture — and the lifted function then read its own not-yet-assigned C local:
#
#   define b(d) as: print of x / local x is 7 / return d
#   VM   100 then 105        AOT  0 then 105        both build, both rc 0
#
# That is worse than the write shape its sibling covers: the write at least
# produced a plausible number, this reads a slot that was never written.
#
# It is also the reason a fix aimed at one refusal has to be checked against the
# other: both read the same `own` map, and nothing in the outward-mutation path
# hints that a capture decision depends on it.
#
# WHICH GUARD FIRES: the bug was a suppressed CAPTURE, but the refusal that
# catches it is the OUTWARD-MUTATION one — once the declaration no longer
# dominates, `local x is 7` is just an assignment to an enclosing name, and that
# check runs first. The first version of this header asserted a capture refusal
# and the refusal tier's substring contract FAILED it — the gate catching a
# false claim about its own subject. The correction was then lost once, because
# it rode in the same compound command as a cd that failed, and the suite
# failed on it a second time before it landed.

define f(y) as:
    local x is 100
    define b(d) as:
        print of x
        local x is 7
        return d
    local r is b of 5
    return x + r

print of (f of 0)
